/**

@mainpage Colman – Library and Collection Manager

@tableofcontents

The end-user documentation is the tutorial in `doc/tutorial/<lang>/`
(German and English, target `tutorial_<lang>`), published on GitHub Pages
at https://pesch-1.github.io/colman/ by `.github/workflows/tutorial.yml`.
Configuring with `-DCOLMAN_DOCS_ONLY=ON` provides only the `doc` and
`tutorial*` targets and needs no Qt or other build dependency.

Colman is a Qt 6/SQLite application for managing school libraries and
other collections. Its core functionality covers the management of
media (items), persons (borrowers), as well as lendings, returns,
acquisitions, liquidations and reservations. Besides the desktop
application there are a REST server, a Qt REST client sharing the
desktop UI, and a Flutter client for mobile devices (`flutter/`).

Supported platforms: the desktop application, REST server and client and
the command-line tools currently run on Linux only (including the
Raspberry Pi); the Flutter client runs on Android.  Windows and macOS
are not supported yet.

@section sec_build Build & Start

@code
cmake -B bld && cmake --build bld -j4
./bld/colman
@endcode

Required Qt modules: `Core Concurrent Gui HttpServer Svg SvgWidgets
Widgets PrintSupport Sql Network Xml Test LinguistTools`. The desktop
application and the REST client additionally need `Multimedia`,
`WebChannel`, `WebEngineWidgets` and ZXing-C++ (camera barcode
scanning, Amazon cover browser); without them only the headless
targets (REST server, tools, tests) are built (`COLMAN_BUILD_GUI`).
Further system dependencies: `libargon2` (password hashing), OpenSSL
(jwt-cpp, fetched via CMake FetchContent).  SQLite comes with
SQLite3MultipleCiphers, which CMake fetches as a pinned release
(FetchContent, checked by SHA-256) and links statically; see
@ref sec_sqlite_driver.
The minimum supported Qt version is 6.4.2; newer Qt APIs must be
guarded with `QT_VERSION_CHECK`. Database files have the extension
`.col`.

The project version (currently 2.0.1) is defined in `CMakeLists.txt`
and is available in the code via the compiler macro `COLMAN_VERSION`.
Builds with GCC/Clang use `-Wall -Wextra`; the code base is kept free
of warnings.

The GitHub Actions CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) builds and tests on
Ubuntu 24.04 with Qt 6.4.2 (the minimum supported Qt version), checks
that every file of the repository is listed in `COLMAN_DIST_FILES`, and
runs `flutter analyze` and `flutter test`.

Generated files that must **not** be edited manually: `moc_*.cpp`,
`qrc_*.cpp`, `*.qm`, and in the Flutter client
`flutter/lib/l10n/app_localizations*.dart`. There are no Qt Designer
`.ui` files; the whole UI is written in C++.

@section sec_layout Source layout

| Directory | Contents |
|---|---|
| `src/` | All C++ sources and headers, Qt resource files (`.qrc`) |
| `tests/` | Unit tests (`*_test.cpp`) and `colman_test_helpers.hpp` |
| `localization/` | Translation sources (`colman_de.ts`) |
| `doc/` | Developer documentation and tutorials |
| `tools/` | Helper scripts (tutorial screenshots, UI pixel check) |
| `cmake/` | CMake helper scripts |
| `flutter/` | Flutter client; its icons are shared with the desktop app |

`src/` is on the include path, so includes use plain
file names (`#include "colman_item.hpp"`).

@section sec_cmake CMake targets

**Libraries**

| Target | Description |
|---|---|
| `colman_core` | Static library with all non-UI sources (entities, SQL layer, barcodes, lookups, …) |
| `colman_rest` | Static library with the REST support (`RestQueries`, auth middleware, JWT validation) |

**Applications and tools**

| Target | Description |
|---|---|
| `colman` | Desktop application |
| `colman_rest_server` | REST API server (headless; convenience target `server`) |
| `colman_rest_client` | REST client with the full desktop UI |
| `colman_transfer` | Command-line import/export of persons (CSV) and items (JSON), see `colman_admin_tools.dox` |
| `colman_gen_testdb` | Fills a `.col` file with random items for performance tests |
| `colman_query_isbn` | Command-line ISBN lookup tool |
| `colman_label_layout_app` | Interactive test for `LabelLayoutWidget` |
| `colman_label_selection_app` | Interactive test for `LabelSelectionWidget` |
| `colman_calibrate_printer` | Interactive printer calibration test |
| `colman_test_label_printer` | Interactive thermal printer test |

**Tests** (run with `ctest --test-dir bld`; network tests only with
`COLMAN_RUN_NETWORK_TESTS=1`)

| Target | Covers |
|---|---|
| `colman_actions_test` | Barcode action classification |
| `colman_db_util_test` | Database utilities (encryption detection, thumbnail paths, column names) |
| `colman_sqlite_driver_test` | Built-in SQLite driver: value conversion, navigation, transactions, schema, encryption |
| `colman_queries_test` | SQL layer and view queries |
| `colman_reservations_test` | Reservations |
| `colman_permission_deps_test` | Permission dependencies and delegation |
| `colman_class_management_test` | Class renaming for a new school year, class list merging |
| `colman_passwords_test` | Password hashing |
| `colman_cutter_test` | Cutter/signature computation |
| `colman_json_test` | JSON serialization |
| `colman_isbn_test`, `colman_bgg_test`, `colman_name_test` | Internet lookups |
| `colman_auth_middleware_test`, `colman_jwt_validator_test` | REST authentication |
| `colman_rest_queries_test`, `colman_rest_authorization_test`, `colman_rest_stress_test` | REST client/server round trips, permissions, concurrency |
| `colman_mdns_announcer_test` | mDNS server discovery |

**Other targets**

| Target | Description |
|---|---|
| `lupdate_colman` | Update the `.ts` files of the main application |
| `doc` | Doxygen documentation (HTML + PDF) |
| `tutorial_<lang>` | Doxygen tutorial for one language (HTML + `tutorial/<lang>/latex/colman_tutorial.pdf`); `tutorial` builds all languages |
| `tutorial_screenshots_<lang>` | Regenerate the tutorial screenshots (see @ref page_tutorial_screenshots) |
| `dist` | Source tarball `colman-<version>.tar.gz` (contents: `COLMAN_DIST_FILES`) |
| `deploy` | Self-contained binary tarball (requires linuxdeploy in `tools/`) |

@section sec_resources Qt resources

| Resource file (in `src/`) | Contents |
|---|---|
| `colman_owls.qrc` | Owl icon set (start page, central page, dialogs) |
| `colman_thirdparty.qrc` | Third-party logos ("Powered by BGG") |
| `colman_app_translations.qrc` | Translations: `colman_de.qm` |

The image files live in `flutter/assets/icons/` and
`flutter/assets/thirdparty/`, so that the Flutter client can use them
as assets. The `.qrc` files refer to them via aliases, which keeps the
resource paths stable (`:/icons/owls/<name>.jpeg`).

@section sec_entry Entry point

`main()` in `colman_main.cpp`:
1. Sets the QApplication metadata (`cpesch`/`colman`)
2. Evaluates the developer hooks `--ui-snapshot=<dir>` (before the
   translations are loaded, so that the pixel baseline stays
   language-independent) and `--tutorial-screenshots=<dir>` (see
   @ref page_tutorial_screenshots; redirects QSettings to a temporary
   directory)
3. Loads the Qt system translations and the application translations
   (`colman_de.qm`) from embedded Qt resources
4. Parses the command line (`QCommandLineParser`): `--help`,
   `--trace-level <number>`, optional `.col` file as positional argument
5. Applies the stored font size and installs the font size shortcut
   filter
6. Creates the central AppData instance, sets the thumbnail directory
   and the system network proxy
7. Builds the main window (`MainWindowUi::setup_ui()`) and calls
   `setup_start_page()` and `setup_preferences_page()`; all other pages
   are set up once a collection has been opened (in
   `colman_start_page.cpp`)
8. Shows the main window and starts the Qt event loop

@subsection sec_trace Trace level and console output

colman is silent by default. Diagnostic output goes through Qt logging
categories from `colman_trace.hpp`, whose debug output is disabled by
default:

| Category | Macro | Active from `--trace-level` | Contents |
|---|---|---|---|
| `colman.lookup` | `qCDebug(colman_lookup_log)` | 2 (`LookupTraceLevel`) | Internet lookup: ISBN/EAN/title, SRU, HTTP errors |

`colman::set_trace_level()` sets the filter rules via
`QLoggingCategory::setFilterRules()` (including the suppression of the
KDirModel warnings). Write new diagnostic output with `qCDebug()` and a
suitable category, not with `qDebug()`. During development a category
can also be enabled via the environment variable
`QT_LOGGING_RULES="colman.lookup.debug=true"` (this also works in
programs without `--trace-level`, e.g. `colman_query_isbn`).
`colman_rest_server` and `colman_rest_client` offer the same
`--trace-level` option and use `colman::set_trace_level(QString const&)`
for it (which validates the input).

---

@page page_architecture Architecture

@section sec_appdata Central state: AppData

colman::AppData (`colman_app_data.hpp/.cpp`) is the **only** state
container of the application. It is passed by reference to all
functions – there are no global or file-local mutable variables (only
constants are allowed at file scope).

Important public members:

| Member | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `queries_` | `std::unique_ptr<IQueries>` | Data access: `SQLQueries` (local `.col` file) or `RestQueries` (REST client) |
| `filename_` | `QString` | Path of the opened `.col` file |
| `current_data_collector_` | `int` | Logged-in user: `-1` = not logged in, `0` = administrator, `>0` = `persons.id` |
| `mw_` | `MainWindowUi` | Hand-written main window UI (see @ref sec_pages) |
| `w_` | `QMainWindow` | Main window widget |
| `isbn_queries_`, `ean_queries_`, `bgg_queries_`, `name_queries_` | `IsbnQueries`, `EanQueries`, `BggQueries`, `NameQueries` | Internet lookups by ISBN, EAN, BoardGameGeek ID and title |
| `show_external_id_ui_` | `bool` | `true` in REST client mode (shows OIDC-related fields) |

**Programmatically created widgets:** pages whose layout is built at
runtime (start page, central page, some sub-pages) keep their widgets
as AppData members so that other parts of the code can access them,
e.g. `open_button_`, `new_button_`, `recently_opened_` (start page),
the central page tiles `new_person_button_` … `scan_button_`, and
`class_management_page_`, `camera_scan_page_`,
`search_item_detail_page_`.

In addition, AppData holds the view model instances of the pages –
`QAbstractTableModel` subclasses from `colman_table_models.hpp`, e.g.
`person_model_`, `lending_person_model_`, `lending_item_model_`,
`return_lending_model_`, `combined_history_model_` – together with
their pagination state (`*_offset_`, `*_has_more_`).

@section sec_pages Page architecture

The UI is based on the `QStackedWidget` `MainWindowUi::pages_stack_`.
`MainWindowUi` (`colman_main_window_ui.hpp`) is written by hand; its
`setup_ui()` is split into `colman_main_window_ui.cpp` and one file per
flow (`colman_main_window_ui_lending.cpp`, `_return`, `_acquisition`,
`_liquidation`, `_management`). Each page module offers up to three
kinds of functions:

- **`setup_<name>_page(s)(QApplication&, AppData&)`** – once: UI
  connections, model initialization
- **`enter_<name>_page(AppData&)`** – on page switch: set focus, load
  data
- **`refresh_<name>_page(s)(AppData&)`** – after data changes: reload
  models and views

`setup_start_page()` and `setup_preferences_page()` are called from
`main()`; all other `setup_*` functions run once a collection has been
opened (`colman_start_page.cpp`). The REST client calls the same
functions from `colman_rest_client_main.cpp`.

**Page overview:**

| Module | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `colman_start_page` | Logo, open/create collection, recently used, login (`AdminLoginPage`, `UserLoginPage`, `DbPasswordPage`), due-returns reminder |
| `colman_create_collection_page` | Step-by-step flow for creating a new collection |
| `colman_central_page` | Central navigation page after login; own password; barcode scanner dispatch |
| `colman_lending_page` | Guided lending flow (also reservations) |
| `colman_group_lending_page` | Group lending: one copy for each ticked borrower, hand-out list |
| `colman_return_page` | Guided return flow |
| `colman_acquisition_page` | Guided acquisition flow (create item, internet lookup, number of copies) |
| `colman_liquidation_page` | Guided liquidation flow |
| `colman_search_page` | Item search with filters, item detail page, list printing and CSV export |
| `colman_history_page` | Transaction history (filter, change, delete) |
| `colman_person_page` | Create/edit persons, permissions, usernames |
| `colman_person_view_page` | Person list |
| `colman_person_barcode_page` | ID card printing |
| `colman_item_barcode_page` | Item label printing |
| `colman_signature_print_page` | Signature (spine) label printing |
| `colman_item_type_page` | Manage media types and their fields |
| `colman_class_management_page` | Class management and new school year |
| `colman_camera_scan_page` | Barcode scanning with a camera |
| `colman_settings_page` | Collection settings (name, logo, lending period, reservations, …) |
| `colman_preferences_page` | Program settings stored in QSettings (internet services and tokens, proxy, thumbnails, timeouts, …) |
| *(dynamic)* | Action selection page when a scanned barcode allows several actions (see @ref sec_barcode_dispatch) |

The editors for ID card, item card and signature label layouts
(`colman_id_editor`, `colman_item_card_editor`,
`colman_signature_label_editor`) are opened from the central page as
pages of the stack as well.

Lending, return, acquisition and liquidation are each implemented as a
single guided multi-step flow; the former split into an "expert" and
an "easy" mode has been removed.

The start page is built entirely programmatically: logo
(`colman_logo.jpeg`), title ("ColMan" + version number), subtitle
("the collection manager"), the buttons open/new/exit and the recently
used table.

@section sec_models View model layer

`colman_table_models.hpp` defines the `QAbstractTableModel` subclasses
that populate the table views of the application. Each class wraps a
`std::vector<Row>` and offers a refresh method that correctly encloses
`beginResetModel()` / `endResetModel()`:

| Class | Usage |
|---|---|
| `TypeListModel` | Media type list |
| `PersonListModel` | Person lists (lending, return, person pages, ID cards) |
| `StartPagePersonListModel` | Login list on the start page (including password flag) |
| `ItemListModel` | Item lists (acquisition, liquidation, lending, labels, search) |
| `OpenLendingListModel` | Open lendings of a person (return flow) |
| `DueLendingListModel` | Due/open lendings (start page reminder, "open lendings" page) |
| `HistoryCombinedModel` | Combined transaction history |
| `HistoryAcquisitionListModel`, `HistoryLiquidationListModel`, `HistoryLendingListModel`, `HistoryReturnListModel` | History per action type |
| `ChangePasswordsPersonListModel` | Admin list for setting/removing passwords |

Each `Row` structure is defined as a public nested struct
(`ItemListModel::Row`, `LendingListModel::Row`, …). Column numbers are
anchored in `data()` and `headerData()` via `switch` statements; the
first column always contains the database ID and is hidden via
`QTableView::setColumnHidden(0, true)`.

`PersonListModel` provides the columns ID, last name, first name, class
(`PersonListModel::ClassColumn`), title (`PersonListModel::TitleColumn`)
and username.  `PersonListWidget::hide_column()` hides columns
permanently (also after every model reset); the `person` and `lending`
pages hide the title this way.
`PersonListWidget` uses the class column for an optional class filter
(`enable_class_filter()`, `set_class_names()`): the combo box sets an
exact regex filter on the `QSortFilterProxyModel`; "all classes"
(invalid item data) removes it, "without class" (empty string) filters
for persons without a class.  The filter is active on the `person`
page; `person_barcode` filters its `QTreeWidget` the same way via
`person_barcode_class_`.

`PersonCheckListWidget` (`colman_person_check_list_widget.hpp`) is a
checkable person list with name search, class filter and "Select all" /
"Deselect all" (acting on the visible rows only; hidden rows keep their
check state).  Rows can be disabled (`set_disabled_ids()`) and annotated
(`set_notes()`); an optional guard (`set_check_guard()`) vetoes checks,
e.g. when no copies are left.  The group lending page and step 2 of the
return page (several returning persons, `IQueries::add_group_return()`)
use it.  Its
capacity rule: borrowers with an own reservation of the item always fit
(they take a reserved copy); all others together may use at most
`available - sum of all reservations` copies.  This matches the check of
`IQueries::add_group_lending()`, which inserts all lendings in one
transaction.

All class filters (person page, ID card printing, open lendings) offer
the union of `load_class_names()` and the classes actually assigned to
persons (`merge_class_names()` in `colman_class_names.hpp`, sorted
numerically via `QCollator`).  The class field in `PersonWidget` still
offers only the class list; `confirm_school_class()` in
`colman_person_page.cpp` asks for confirmation when an unknown class is
saved and, given the `manage_classes` permission, adds it to the list
via `add_class_name()`.

"Without class" in the open lendings is filtered on the client side:
`query_due_lendings()` interprets an empty class name as "no filter",
so the rows are reduced afterwards to persons without a class
(`classless_person_ids_`) – without changes to `IQueries` or the REST
API.

`DueLendingListModel::Row` carries the class separately from the name
(`school_class`, its own column 3 on the "open lendings" page);
`person_with_class()` returns the former "Name (class)" format, which
the start page and `person_name` in `GET /api/lendings/due` continue to
use.

The models are filled by the view query functions of `IQueries` (see
@ref page_sql), which return typed vectors that are loaded directly
into the models, e.g.:

| Function | Usage |
|---|---|
| `query_types()` | Media type list |
| `query_persons()`, `query_lending_persons()` | Person lists (including class) |
| `query_items()`, `query_acquired_items()`, `query_available_items()`, `query_lent_items()`, `query_items_with_type()` | Item lists, paginated via `offset`/`limit` (`count_items()` for the total) |
| `query_items_for_search()` | Search page (full-text search via the FTS5 table `items_fts`) |
| `query_open_lendings()`, `query_item_lendings()`, `query_due_lendings()` | Open, per-item and due lendings |
| `query_combined_history()` | History of all action types, optionally filtered by period and action type |
| `query_*_field_completions()` | Autocomplete suggestions |

`query_combined_history()` accepts an `is_admin` parameter: the name of
the data collector is only included for administrators.

@section sec_barcode_dispatch Barcode scanner dispatch

The central navigation page receives barcode scanner input (USB
keyboard emulation: character sequence + Enter): the page widget
`CentralPage` (in `colman_central_page.cpp`) collects the characters in
`keyPressEvent()` and passes the string on when Enter arrives. The
camera button (`scan_button_`) feeds barcodes decoded via
`scan_barcode()` (`colman_camera_scan_page`) into the same path.
`possible_actions()` (`colman_actions.cpp`) classifies the barcode by
the collection-specific prefix (or as ISBN/EAN) and returns a list of
`ActionInfo` structures.

`dispatch_actions()` branches depending on the length of the list:

| Number of actions | Behavior |
|---|---|
| 0 | No reaction |
| 1 | `dispatch_single_action()` → navigate directly to the matching page |
| >1 | `dispatch_multiple_actions()` → show the action selection page |

**`ActionInfo` fields** (in `colman_actions.hpp`):

| Field | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `action_` | `Action` | `None`, `Acquisition`, `Liquidation`, `Lending`, `Return` |
| `item_id_` | `int` | DB ID of the identified item (-1 = unknown) |
| `person_id_` | `int` | DB ID of the person (-1 = unknown) |
| `lending_id_` | `int` | DB ID of the associated lending (-1 = unknown) |
| `acquisition_id_` | `int` | DB ID of the associated acquisition (-1 = unknown) |
| `barcode_` | `QString` | Scanned barcode string (e.g. ISBN) |

**`dispatch_single_action()`** calls `enter_*_for_action()`:

| Action | Function |
|---|---|
| `Acquisition` | `enter_acquisition_page_for_action()` |
| `Lending` | `enter_lend_page_for_action()` |
| `Return` | `enter_return_page_for_action()` |
| `Liquidation` | `enter_liquidation_page_for_action()` |

Each of these functions navigates to the matching page and sets
preselections (person, item, lending) based on the IDs contained in
`ActionInfo`. Reactive query refreshes ensure that dependent tables are
filtered immediately.

**`dispatch_multiple_actions()`** creates a dynamic selection page
(object name `"action_selection_page"` in the `QStackedWidget`):

- Back button (full width) → central page
- Heading "What do you want to do?" (bold)
- Scrollable list of `ActionButton` entries (icon, bold title,
  multi-line description), one per action, with the owl icon of the
  action (`action_icon_path()`) and a descriptive text
  (`action_description()`):
  - **Return:** item name — author, borrower name, lending date
  - **Lending:** item name — author
  - **Acquisition:** barcode (ISBN)
  - **Liquidation:** item name — author

Clicking an entry calls `dispatch_single_action()` for the selected
action. On the next call of `dispatch_multiple_actions()` the existing
selection page is first removed from the stack and deleted.

@section sec_login Login & user management

After a database file has been opened, the user must authenticate:

- **Encrypted collections** first ask for the database password
  (`DbPasswordPage`).
- **Administrator login** (`AdminLoginPage`): password page with a
  3-second lock after a failed attempt. Sets
  `data.current_data_collector_ = 0`.
- **User login** (`UserLoginPage`): person list + password field, also
  with a 3-second lock. Sets `data.current_data_collector_ = person_id`.
- If no password is set, password entry is skipped.

All login pages are pages of the stack in `colman_start_page.cpp`.
What a user may do is decided by the permissions of the logged-in
person (`has_permission()`, table `permissions`, see
@ref page_database); they control the visibility of the tiles on the
central page. `current_data_collector_` is also stamped as data
collector on all mutating operations.

**Layout of the central page** (`setup_central_page()`): the tiles are
arranged in four row widgets (lending desk, stock, persons and own
account, setup), each a left-aligned `QHBoxLayout`. Hidden tiles leave
no gap; rows without a visible tile are hidden each time the page is
entered. `equalize_button_sizes()` sets all tiles (including hidden
ones) to the width of the widest and the height of the highest via
`setFixedSize()`, so that the columns stay aligned even with long
translations and tiles with one- and two-line labels are equally high;
it runs again after every label change (lend/reserve) and on font size
changes. The screenshot scenario checks the sizes and the alignment
automatically (`check_central_grid()`).

**Passwords** are hashed with Argon2 (`colman_passwords.hpp`,
`colman::hash_password()` / `colman::verify_password()`). The hashes
are stored in the `credentials` table of the respective database file.
The same table also stores the REST API username and the external OIDC
ID (column `external_id`), so that the desktop password and the REST
login are always in sync.

Users change their **own password** via `change_own_password()`
(`IQueries`): the current password is checked against the stored hash
before the new (already hashed) value is set. Over REST this is the
endpoint `POST /api/me/password` (`{old_password, new_password}`, since
API minor 21) — the only password endpoint without
`may_manage_target`, because the caller proves knowledge of the current
password. Setting/deleting *other users'* passwords remains reserved to
`POST`/`DELETE /api/persons/<id>/password` (admin list view in the
Flutter client `/api/persons/change-passwords`).

---

@page page_entities Domain entities

All entities are simple data classes with getters/setters in the
`colman` namespace.

@section sec_collection Collection

`colman_collection.hpp` – collection-wide settings.

Important fields: `name_`, `organization_`, `responsible_`,
`logo_raw_` (QByteArray, original raw data — SVG, PNG or another
format; rendered on demand for the screen and at full printer
resolution for printing), `barcode_prefix_`, `data_version_`,
`max_person_id_`, `use_lending_period_` and `lending_period_*`,
`allow_reservations_`, `reservation_period_days_`,
`default_item_type_`, `default_is_school_item_`,
`grade_range_min_`/`grade_range_max_` (grade levels for school items)
and `server_name_` (name announced for LAN discovery).  The fields `id_card_layout_`,
`item_card_layout_` and `sig_label_layout_` still exist, but are only
filled on loading from legacy entries, if present (read migration into
local presets), and are no longer written back to the database —
layouts are stored locally per client.

Methods for SVG logo support:
- `has_svg_logo()` — checks whether `logo_raw_` contains SVG data
- `get_logo_raw()` — returns the raw data for printing
- `set_logo_raw(arr)` — sets the raw data (format auto-detected)

Helper functions for barcode computation and parsing:
`compute_item_barcode()`, `parse_item_barcode()`,
`compute_person_barcode()`, `parse_person_barcode()`.
`add_lending_period()` computes the default due date.

@section sec_type Type

`colman_type.hpp` – defines a user-defined media type with its own
database table and dynamic attributes.

- `name_` – display name
- `tablename_` – SQLite table name
- `keys_` – ordered list of attributes as `KeyVector`

Attribute types (`DataTypeInfo`): `String`, `ShortString`, `Date`,
`Time`, `Integer`, `Float`, `Duration`, `Table`, `IntegerRanges`.

An attribute can be marked as repeatable via the `"is_repeatable"` key
in `DataTypeInfo::info_`.  Repeatable fields may contain several values
(e.g. several subject keywords) and are stored in the database in the
`item_list_values` table instead of the type-specific table.  The
function `colman::is_repeatable(DataTypeInfo)` checks this flag.  The
type editor shows the column "Repeatable" (German: "Wiederholbar").

@section sec_item Item

`colman_item.hpp` – a physical item (e.g. a book).

- `name_`, `barcode_`, `place_`, `description_`, `keywords_`
- `type_id_` – foreign key to Type
- `values_` – dynamic type attributes as `StringMap` (single values)
- `list_values_` – repeatable type attributes as `StringListMap`
  (several values per key; persisted in `item_list_values`)
- `is_school_item_`, `subject_`, `academic_year_` – school book fields
- `signature_top_`, `signature_bottom_` – upper and lower signature
  label (spine labels; stored in the database as `signature_top` and
  `signature_bottom`). The acquisition flow remembers the signatures of
  the most recently created item per collection in QSettings
  (`acquisition_signature/<hex(name + barcode prefix)>/top|bottom`) and
  sets them as default via `ItemWidget::set_default_signatures()`;
  internet lookup results are applied via
  `ItemWidget::lookup_result_to_fields()`, so that only a non-empty
  looked-up signature replaces the default.
- `thumbnail_id_` – image in the database
- `allow_reservations_`, `reservation_period_days_` – per-item
  reservation settings (`-1` = inherit from the collection)
- `data_collector_id_` – ID of the user who entered the record
  (0 = administrator)
- `version_` – optimistic locking counter (REST ETags)

@section sec_person Person

`colman_person.hpp` – a borrower.

- `lastname_`, `firstname_`, `title_`, `school_class_`
- `barcode_`
- `emails_`, `telephone_`, address fields, `remarks_`
- `username_`, `external_id_` – login data (stored in `credentials`)
- `thumbnail_id_`
- `data_collector_id_` – ID of the user who entered the record
- `version_` – optimistic locking counter (REST ETags)

@section sec_transactions Transaction entities

| Entity | File | Purpose | Key fields |
|---|---|---|---|
| `Lending` | `colman_lending.hpp` | Lending | `item_id_`, `person_id_`, `count_`, `date_`, `duedate_` |
| `Return` | `colman_return.hpp` | Return | `lending_id_`, `count_`, `date_` |
| `Acquisition` | `colman_acquisition.hpp` | Acquisition (stock increase) | `item_id_`, `count_`, `date_` |
| `Liquidation` | `colman_liquidation.hpp` | Liquidation (stock decrease) | `item_id_`, `count_`, `date_` |

All four transaction entities also have a `data_collector_id_` field
that records the user who entered them.

**Stock computation:**
@code
stock_size() = sum(acquisitions) - sum(liquidations)
available()  = stock_size()      - sum(open lendings)
@endcode

---

@page page_sql SQL layer

All data access goes through the abstract interface `colman::IQueries`
(`colman_iqueries.hpp`). It has two implementations:

- `SQLQueries` (`colman_sql_queries.hpp`) – direct SQLite access to a
  local `.col` file; used by the desktop application and the REST
  server. The implementation is split by topic into
  `colman_sql_queries.cpp` (database file, encryption, collection,
  backup), `colman_sql_queries_types.cpp`, `_items.cpp`,
  `_persons.cpp`, `_movements.cpp` (acquisitions, liquidations,
  lendings, returns, reservations) and `_history.cpp` (history, class
  management); shared helpers are in `colman_sql_queries_impl.hpp`.
- `RestQueries` (`colman_rest_queries.hpp`) – forwards the calls to a
  `colman_rest_server` over HTTP; used by `colman_rest_client`.

There are no SQL queries outside `colman_sql_queries*.cpp`.

@section sec_sqlite_driver Built-in SQLite driver

`SQLQueries` does not use Qt's `QSQLITE` plugin but colman's own Qt SQL
driver (`colman_sqlite_driver.hpp`), registered as `COLMAN_SQLITE` by
`register_sqlite_driver()`.  It is built on SQLite3MultipleCiphers
(linked statically with hidden symbols) and uses only the public QtSql
API (`QSqlDriver`, `QSqlResult`), so encryption is always available and
no plugin or private Qt header is involved.

- Values are bound and converted like in Qt's `QSQLITE` driver (e.g.
  `QDateTime` as ISO 8601 text with milliseconds, NULL as a null
  `QString` variant).  Named placeholders are converted to positional
  ones by `QSqlResult`.
- Rows are read as the query advances; moving backwards re-executes the
  statement.  `QSqlQuery::size()` is not supported (-1), as with
  `QSQLITE`.
- Encrypted files use ChaCha20-Poly1305; keys are passed as UTF-8.
  `set_database_key()` unlocks a database right after opening,
  `change_database_key()` encrypts, re-encrypts or (with an empty key)
  decrypts the open database in place.

@section sec_sql_patterns Function patterns

The functions of `IQueries` follow the scheme:

- `create_*` / `add_new_*` / `add_*` – insert
- `load_*` / `get_*` / `read_*` – read
- `save_*` / `change_*` / `set_*` – update
- `remove_*` – delete
- `query_*` – view queries returning typed `std::vector<Row>` values
  for the table models (see @ref sec_models)
- `change_*_versioned()` – updates with optimistic locking (version
  check), used by the REST server for ETags

@section sec_sql_groups Function groups

**Database file:** `create_new_database()` (optionally encrypted),
`add_administrator()`, `load_database()`, `save_database()`,
`create_tables()`; on opening, `load_database()` applies schema
migrations (see @ref page_database)

**Encryption:** `supports_encryption()` (always true for
`SQLQueries`), `is_encrypted()`, `encrypt_database()`,
`change_encryption_key()`, `remove_encryption()`; implemented with the
built-in driver (see @ref sec_sqlite_driver)

**Collection:** `create_collection()`, `change_collection()`,
`read_collection()`; signature categories:
`read_signature_top_categories()`, `add_/remove_signature_*_category()`

**Types:** `add_new_type()`, `get_type()`, `get_type_id()`,
`get_type_name()`, `get_type_locale()`, `change_type()`,
`remove_type()`, `type_id_for_media_kind()` /
`media_kind_for_type_id()`, field order
(`get_combined_field_order()`, `save_field_order()`)

**Items:** `add_new_item()`, `get_item()`, `change_item()`,
`remove_item()`, `force_remove_item()`, `restrict_item_to_type()`,
`items_by_isbn()`, `items_by_ean()`

**Computed queries for items:** `available()`, `stock_size()`,
`have_lendings()`, `get_recently_acquired_item_ids()` (the most
recently acquired items) and `get_items_acquired_between()` (items
acquired within a period) — both drive the filters of label printing
(`colman_item_barcode_page`) and are also available via the REST
endpoints `/api/items/recently-acquired` and
`/api/items/acquired-between`, respectively.

**Persons:** `add_new_person()`, `get_person()`, `change_person()`,
`remove_person()`, `person_known()`, `email_known()`,
`username_known()`, `lending_balance()`,
`get_person_by_external_id()`, `set_person_external_id()`

**Acquisitions / Liquidations / Lendings / Returns:**
`add_new_*()`, `get_*()`, `change_*()`, `remove_*()` each

**Reservations:** `add_reservation()`, `remove_reservation()`,
`get_reservations_for_item()`, `get_reservation()`,
`get_reservations()`

**Permissions:** `get_permissions()`, `get_user_permissions()`,
`create_default_permissions()`, `has_permission()`,
`set_permission()`, `remove_permissions()`, `is_reserve_only()`;
delegation rules in `colman_permission_deps.hpp`

**Class management:** `load_class_names()`, `add_class_name()`,
`remove_class_name()`, `rename_class_name()`,
`apply_school_year_changes()`

**Concurrency:** `get_version()`, `change_*_versioned()`,
`query_table_versions()` (change counters per table, maintained by
triggers; clients poll them to refresh their views)

**Extending due dates:** `extend_lendings(ids, duedate)` sets the due
date of several lendings in one transaction (REST:
`POST /api/lendings/extend`, permission `collect_lendings`).  The
desktop UI for this is `colman_extend_lending_dialog.hpp` with two
entry points following the rule "the dialog only asks for what is not
yet known": `extend_lendings_of_person()` (only the person is known —
lending step 1; selection + date) and `extend_selected_lendings()` (the
lendings are known — selected rows on the "open lendings" page, the
lending chosen in return step 3; date only).  The proposed date never
shortens a due date: max(today, latest current due date) plus the
default lending period (`Collection::add_lending_period()`), or plus 14
days without a default lending period (`propose_due_date()` in
`colman_extend_lending_dialog.cpp`).
The Flutter client follows the same rule
(`flutter/lib/widgets/extend_lending_dialog.dart`):
`showExtendLendingDialog()` in the person step of the lending,
`showExtendSelectedLendingsDialog()` in the details step of the return
and in the selection mode of `OpenLendingsScreen` (button "Extend due
date" in the bottom bar or a long press on a row; in selection mode
"Cancel" and "Extend (n)" in the same bar); the proposed date comes
from `CollectionInfo.proposeExtendedDueDate()` (same rule as
`propose_due_date()`), the preset due date for new lendings from
`CollectionInfo.addLendingPeriod()` (same end-of-month rule as
`QDate::addMonths`).  For the list of open lendings,
`GET /api/lendings/due` therefore also accepts `collect_lendings`.

**History:** `query_combined_history()` combines all action types via
`UNION ALL`; the `action_filter` parameter (e.g. `"lending"`) restricts
the query to one type (REST: query parameter `action`; Flutter:
drop-down in the history). `apply_action_change()` and
`batch_delete_history_actions()` change or delete recorded actions.

**Credentials (desktop & REST):** `set_password()`, `get_password()`,
`has_password()`, `remove_password()`, `change_own_password()`,
`check_local_credentials()`, `set_local_credentials()`,
`delete_local_credentials()`, `get_username()`, `set_username()`,
`remove_username()`

All credential data — desktop password hash, REST username/password
hash and OIDC `external_id` — are stored together in the `credentials`
table (one record per person). UPSERT syntax keeps the columns
independently updatable.

**Thumbnails:** `store_thumbnail()`, `get_thumbnail()`,
`use_local_thumbnail()`, `have_local_thumbnail()`

**Barcodes:** `compute_item_barcode()`, `parse_item_barcode()`,
`is_item_barcode()`, `compute_person_barcode()`,
`parse_person_barcode()`, `is_person_barcode()` (see
@ref sec_barcodes)

---

@page page_backup Auto backup

`SQLQueries` writes automatic backups of the opened `.col` file:

- `backup_once()` – copies the database file next to it with a
  numeric suffix (e.g. `school.col.~3~`). If there are more than 20
  backup files, old ones are removed (at least 10 are kept); an
  optional callback can ask the user first.
- `backup_after_change()` – called by all mutating operations: writes
  one backup per session before the first change, then counts the
  changes and writes another backup once the threshold is reached
  (QSettings key `changes_until_backup`, default 16).

In-memory databases (no file path) are not backed up.

---

@page page_errors Error handling

@section sec_try_catch COLMAN_TRY / COLMAN_CATCH

`colman_try_catch.hpp` – macro-based exception handling for UI event
handlers:

@code
void MyPage::on_button_clicked()
{
  COLMAN_TRY
  {
    // database operations that may throw exceptions
  }
  COLMAN_CATCH;  // catches colman::Error and std::exception,
                  // shows a dialog, writes to std::cerr
}
@endcode

Variants: `COLMAN_INT_CATCH` (sets an int variable),
`COLMAN_STRING_CATCH` (stores the error message as a string).

@section sec_error colman::Error

`colman_error.hpp` – custom exception class.

@code
throw colman::Error("Record not found", COLMAN_HERE);
@endcode

`COLMAN_HERE` expands to file name + line number.

@section sec_assert COLMAN_ASSERT / COLMAN_VERIFY

`colman_assert.hpp` – multi-level assertion framework
(Alexandrescu & Torjo pattern):

| Macro | Active in | Behavior |
|---|---|---|
| `COLMAN_ASSERT(expr)` | Debug mode | Configurable per level |
| `COLMAN_VERIFY(expr)` | Always | Throws an exception (default) |

Levels: `.warn()` (continue), `.debug()` (ask the user),
`.error()` (exception), `.fatal()` (abort).

Values can be attached for diagnostics:

@code
COLMAN_ASSERT(x > 0)(x).msg("x must be positive").error();
@endcode

---

@page page_barcodes Barcodes & printing

@section sec_barcodes Barcode system

- Barcodes are collection-specific (prefix from
  `Collection::barcode_prefix_`)
- Items: `compute_item_barcode(collection, type_id, item_id)`
- Persons: `compute_person_barcode(collection, person_id)`
- Barcodes are never stored but always computed.
- Code128 rendering: `colman_code128_item.hpp` – `Code128Item`
  (`QGraphicsItem` subclass); `adapt_size(width, height, width_unit)`
  adapts the bar width to the available zone (clamps to
  0.95 × width / rl_sum_ if `width_unit` is too large)

@subsection sec_barcode_formats Barcode formats

All colman barcodes are pure digit strings (`P` = 5-digit prefix,
`t`/`i` = number of digits of the following type/item/person ID,
always ≥ 1):

| Format | Kind   | Structure           | Check digit                     | Length |
|--------|--------|---------------------|---------------------------------|--------|
| 1      | Item   | `P t T i I c`       | Σ(position · digit) mod 10      | 9+t+i  |
| 1      | Person | `P 0 i I c`         | Σ(position · digit) mod 10      | 8+i    |
| 2      | Item   | `P 00 t T i I c'`   | (GTIN check digit + 5) mod 10   | 10+t+i |
| 2      | Person | `P 00 0 i I c'`     | (GTIN check digit + 5) mod 10   | 10+i   |

Only format 2 is generated; both formats are read, so that labels and
ID cards that have already been printed remain valid. Format 1
occasionally collides with ISBN/EAN: e.g. `5486111410276` (item 1027)
is also a valid EAN-13. Format 2 rules this out by construction:

- The GTIN check digit (weights 3, 1, 3, … from the right) is identical
  for EAN-8, UPC-A, EAN-13/ISBN-13 and GTIN-14. `c'` always differs from
  it, so a format 2 barcode is an invalid GTIN at any length.
- Format 2 has at least 11 digits and therefore cannot be an
  ISBN-10/ISSN.
- The marker `00` after the prefix never occurs in format 1. Older
  colman versions therefore reject format 2 instead of misinterpreting
  it. Server and clients must be updated together (REST API 2.2).

Distinction in `parse_*_barcode()` (digits `d1 d2 d3` after the
prefix): `d1 ≥ 1` → format 1 item; `d1 = 0, d2 ≥ 1` → format 1 person;
`000` → format 2 person; `00`, `d3 ≥ 1` → format 2 item. Each format
only checks its own check digit. The Flutter client computes person
barcodes locally (`computePersonBarcode` in
`flutter/lib/models/id_card_layout.dart`); this function must match
`compute_person_barcode()`.

@section sec_id_card ID card layout editor

`colman_id_editor.hpp/.cpp` – interactive ID card layout editor:

- Three movable and resizable zones: **barcode**, **text**,
  **logo** (optional)
- **Resizing**: eight handles (corners + edge midpoints) on each zone;
  the logo zone only has four corner handles (the aspect ratio is
  preserved)
- **Double-clicking a zone** automatically expands it to the largest
  possible area within the card boundaries without overlapping other
  zones. For the logo zone the aspect ratio of the logo is preserved;
  the barcode and text zones fill the maximum rectangular area
  completely.
- The layout is held as an `IdCardLayout` struct and persisted
  **locally per client** (desktop: `QSettings`; Flutter:
  `IdCardLayoutStore`), since different printers may be attached to
  each client. It is **no longer** stored in the database or sent to
  the server via the REST API. Existing
  `id_card_*`/`item_card_*`/`sig_lbl_*` entries in old `.col` files are
  taken over into the local presets once on opening
  (`migrate_card_layouts`) and are not written afterwards.
- The text zone shows four lines: collection name, organization, issue
  date, person name (bold)
- When printing (`colman_person_barcode_page`), the SVG logo is
  rendered directly as `QGraphicsSvgItem` + `QSvgRenderer` — so it
  appears at full printer DPI instead of as a scaled raster copy

**Data types** (`colman_id_card_layout.hpp`):

`IdCardElementType` – zone type: `Barcode`, `Text`, `Logo`

`IdCardElement` – position and size of a zone:

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `type_` | Zone type |
| `orientation_` | Rotation angle in degrees (0 / 90 / 180 / 270) |
| `x_mm_` / `y_mm_` | Position of the top-left corner (mm) |
| `width_mm_` / `height_mm_` | Zone dimensions (mm) |

`IdCardLayout` – complete card layout:

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `card_width_mm_` / `card_height_mm_` | Card dimensions (mm); width = longer side |
| `barcode_` / `text_` / `logo_` | Zone descriptions |
| `has_logo_` | `true` if a logo zone is configured |

`card_width_mm_ == 0` means: no layout configured yet.

@section sec_print Printing

Print preview via `QPrintPreviewDialog`. Reports are generated as HTML
tables. `colman_preview_file_dialog.hpp` – extended file dialog with
preview.

The search page (`colman_search_page`) adds a configurable list print
and a Koha-compatible CSV export with MARC21 column headers.

SVG logos are automatically rendered at the printer's resolution when
printing (`Qt6::SvgWidgets`). On screen, a rasterized `QPixmap` from
`Collection::logo_` is still used.

---

@page page_labels Label printing

@section sec_label_entry_points Entry points

The label printing page (`colman_item_barcode_page`) can be reached
from the central navigation page. In addition,
`enter_item_barcode_page_for_item(data, item_id, back)` jumps directly
to the page with the item already checked; the `back` callback
determines where the back button leads. Callers are the acquisition
completion page ("Print label") and the item detail page of the search
(`colman_search_page`).

@section sec_label_layout LabelLayoutWidget

`colman_label_layout_widget.hpp` – widget for managing label sheet
layouts.

The data type `colman::LabelLayoutPreset` describes a format:

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `name` | Display name (key for user presets) |
| `columns` / `rows` | Number of columns and rows |
| `label_width` / `label_height` | Label width/height (mm) |
| `left_margin` / `top_margin` | Left/top page margin (mm) |
| `h_gap` / `v_gap` | Horizontal/vertical gap between labels (mm) |
| `page_width_mm` / `page_height_mm` | Printable page size (mm; default A4) |
| `builtin` | `true` = immutable factory preset |
| `is_label_printer`, `printer_cups_name`, `printer_dpi` | Thermal/label printer binding |
| `rotate_180` | Rotate the output by 180° |

`LabelLayoutWidget` offers a combo box with all known layouts (factory
presets + user presets), input fields for the geometry and a live
preview of the A4 page. Clicking **Apply** emits the signal
`layout_applied(LabelLayoutPreset const&)`.

For thermal/label printer presets (`is_label_printer == true`) the
widget shows a separate **printer** combo box listing the printers
detected via `list_thermal_printers()`. This allows changing the printer
bound to a layout (`printer_cups_name` / `printer_dpi`) later on without
losing geometry or arrangement; a printer that is no longer detected is
kept as an entry.

Static helper methods for direct access without a widget instance:
- `builtin_layouts()` – all factory presets (Avery, Herma, Sigel)
- `load_user_layouts()` – user presets from `QSettings`
- `save_user_layout()` / `delete_user_layout()` – persistence

Standalone test app: `colman_label_layout_app` (called without
arguments).

@section sec_label_selection LabelSelectionWidget

`colman_label_selection_widget.hpp` – widget for selecting individual
labels on a label sheet.

The return type `colman::SelectedLabel` identifies a label:

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `col` / `row` | Column/row index (0-based) |
| `x_mm` / `y_mm` | Position of the top-left corner on the page (mm) |
| `width_mm` / `height_mm` | Width and height (mm) |

`LabelSelectionWidget` consists of:
- **Combo box** with layout selection + icon button that opens a
  `LabelLayoutWidget` dialog (allows managing layouts without a context
  switch)
- **QGraphicsView** with clickable label rectangles:
  - white = selectable
  - blue = selected
  - grey + "forbidden" cursor = maximum reached
- **"Deselect all"** and **"Select all"** buttons (the latter fills up
  positions in row-major order until the maximum is reached) + status
  line "N / max selected"

The constructor receives the maximum number of selectable labels:
@code
auto* w = new colman::LabelSelectionWidget(10);
// … embed in a dialog …
QList<colman::SelectedLabel> sel = w->selected_labels();
@endcode

Fewer than the requested number may be selected (the remaining labels
go on a new sheet); more than `max_count` is blocked.

Standalone test app: `colman_label_selection_app [max_count]`
(default: 10). Clicking **Confirm** prints the selection to stdout and
exits the program.

@section sec_thermal_printer Thermal printer support

`colman_label_printer.hpp/.cpp` – printing on thermal printers
(Vevor Y486, Brother QL, Dymo, Zebra, TSC, …): preferably as a raw
TSPL job directly via `/dev/usb/lp*`, otherwise via CUPS.

**Scene coordinates:** 1 scene unit = 0.1 mm (10 units/mm) –
consistent with the rest of colman.

`ThermalPrinterInfo` – describes a detected thermal printer:

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `cups_name` | CUPS-internal name (passed to `QPrinter`) |
| `display_name` | Human-readable model name |
| `default_size_mm` | Default label dimensions (mm); (0,0) if unknown |
| `dpi` | Native resolution (default: 203 dpi) |

**API overview:**

| Function | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `list_thermal_printers()` | All CUPS printers that meet the thermal criteria |
| `thermal_printer_ready(cups_name)` | Checks whether the printer is still available |
| `list_thermal_printer_devices()` | All existing USB printer devices (`/dev/usb/lp*`), vendor-independent |
| `query_paper_status(device_path)` | Paper status of a USB printer device (`PaperStatus`: `Ok`/`Empty`/`Unknown`); first via the USB status byte (`LPGETSTATUS` ioctl), on failure via the TSPL status query `ESC !?` |
| `thermal_printer_paper_status()` | Combined paper status of all connected USB printers; reports `Empty` only if no device reports paper |
| `warn_when_out_of_labels(parent)` | Shows a warning dialog when the label roll is empty; `true` = cancel printing |
| `warn_when_roll_runs_out_while_printing(parent, label_count, size_mm)` | Monitors the printer after a job has been sent and warns if the roll runs out **during** printing |
| `prime_thermal_printer_geometry(size_mm, dpi)` | Transfers the label geometry (EPL2 `Q<height>,<gap>`) to all TSPL-capable USB printers; called by `send_images_to_thermal_printer()` before every CUPS fallback job (the direct TSPL job carries its own geometry) |
| `render_label_scene(scene, size_mm, dpi, cal)` | Renders scene → `QImage` with embedded DPI metadata; the optional `PrinterCalibration cal` (default: identity) shifts/scales the content to correct the print offset |
| `load_printer_calibration(cups_name)` / `save_printer_calibration(...)` | Loads/saves the printer-specific `PrinterCalibration` (offset/scale) from/to QSettings (`colman_printer_calibration.hpp`) |
| `send_image_to_thermal_printer(image, cups_name, size_mm, dpi, error)` | Sends a finished image without a dialog (wrapper around the multi-image variant) |
| `send_images_to_thermal_printer(images, cups_name, size_mm, dpi, error)` | Sends several labels as **one** job: preferably as a raw TSPL job (1-bit threshold, pixel-exact) directly to `/dev/usb/lp*`, otherwise as a CUPS job (pages via `newPage()`); avoids the printer's recalibration/pause between single jobs |
| `print_label_scene(scene, size_mm, cups_name, dpi, error)` | Convenience wrapper: renders and prints in one step |

**Detection:** the thermal criteria are (OR):
- make-and-model or CUPS name contains a keyword (Vevor, Brother QL,
  Dymo, Zebra, TSC, Niimbot, …)
- the printer reports at least one page size whose shorter side is
  between 5 mm and 110 mm

**Paper-out detection:** `query_paper_status()` works in two stages.
First it reads the status byte of the USB printer class (IEEE 1284
convention, bit 5 = "paper empty") via the `LPGETSTATUS` ioctl of the
usblp kernel driver – a passive class request that does not disturb the
data stream. Many label printer firmwares, however, do not answer the
underlying `GET_PORT_STATUS` request (Vevor Y486 and Munbyn RW403B
return `EIO`); in that case the second stage sends the TSPL immediate
status query `ESC !?` and reads back the response byte with a timeout
(bit 2 = "out of paper", verified on the device: 0x00 with roll, 0x07
without). The second stage is an active request into the data stream
and is therefore only triggered immediately before printing labels.
Access requires read permission on `/dev/usb/lp*` (user in group
`lp`); in addition, the nodes disappear while a CUPS job is running
(the CUPS backend detaches usblp) – in both cases the query returns
`Unknown` and printing continues unhindered. All three label printing
paths (`colman_card_batch_print.cpp`, `colman_signature_print_page.cpp`)
call `warn_when_out_of_labels()` before printing and abort if the roll
is empty.

**Direct TSPL printing:** `send_images_to_thermal_printer()` thresholds
each rendered label to 1 bit (50 % grey, bit 0 = black) and writes the
whole batch as **one** raw TSPL job (`SIZE`/`GAP`/`DIRECTION 0`/
`REFERENCE`/`DENSITY`, then `CLS` + `BITMAP` + `PRINT 1` per label) to
the first suitable `/dev/usb/lp*` device: one that answers the TSPL
status query, or a TSPL model known by its USB ID whose firmware never
answers (Munbyn RW403B). Such models are **not queried at all**: the
query bytes end up in their command parser, and the firmware then
discards the entire following job (verified on the RW403B) — which is
why the paper pre-check and form length priming are skipped there.
Unknown silent devices are left to the CUPS fallback (raw TSPL sent to
a Brother QL/Dymo/ZPL printer would be garbage). Reason for the direct
path: the CUPS raster chain (PDF → Ghostscript → `rastertolabel`)
scales the page image by sub-pixel amounts (Qt rounds the page size to
whole points, e.g. 15 mm → 43 pt → 121 px for an image height of
120 px) and rasterizes the resulting grey edges with halftone
dithering — the print looks grainy. The direct path outputs the
rendered image pixel-exactly; the Flutter client prints over USB the
same way. The job is built with the **placement profile** of the target
device (`TsplDeviceProfile`, assigned via the USB vendor/product ID):
the Vevor Y486 needs `REFERENCE -64,0` and bitmap x = 11 mm (constants
of the Flutter client, so that desktop and mobile prints line up), the
Munbyn RW403B and unknown models print edge-exact from the default
origin (`REFERENCE 0,0`, x = 0). The `BITMAP` command uses mode 1 (OR)
— identical to overwriting on the buffer cleared by `CLS`, but the
RW403B firmware silently discards jobs with mode 0 completely (verified
on the device). The print darkness is passed on unchanged as TSPL
`DENSITY` (0–15) (the CUPS driver halved the value). Before closing the
device, the write path (`write_all()`) waits via `poll(POLLOUT)` until
the printer has accepted all bytes: the usblp kernel driver transfers
writes via a single in-flight URB, reports `POLLOUT` exactly when no
write URB is active any more, and cancels a still running URB on
`close()` — without the wait, jobs larger than the 8 kB driver buffer
would be truncated (symptom: only the first label of a batch is
printed). Without an accessible TSPL device (missing permissions,
network printer, foreign firmware) the function falls back to the
previous CUPS path.

**Post-print monitoring:** If the roll runs out **in the middle of a
batch**, the printer pauses with the rest of the job in memory and
continues printing on its own after a new roll has been inserted
(verified on the Y486) — but without a notice the user would not know
why printing stopped. `warn_when_roll_runs_out_while_printing()` is
therefore called by all three label printing paths after a job has been
sent successfully: it polls the TSPL status query every 500 ms (modal
`QProgressDialog` against re-entrancy; cancelling only ends the
monitoring, not the printing) and shows the notice dialog on "out of
paper" (bit 0x04). Measured Y486 behavior on which the logic is based:
while the head is printing, the firmware does **not answer** the status
query at all — silence of an opened device is therefore the "printing"
signal; when the roll runs out mid-job it answers again, with bit 0x04
set (0x24, then 0x06/0x07); after the end of the job it returns 0x00.
The monitoring ends with the first idle response after observed
activity, after three idle responses without any activity (CUPS
fallback, whose job is not running yet) or at the latest at the safety
deadline (3 s + 1 s per label). Responses that are not picked up pile
up in the device FIFO — `tspl_query_status()` therefore drains the read
channel before every query and evaluates the most recent byte. If no
device can be opened (CUPS fallback detaches usblp, missing
permissions), the function returns immediately without a dialog;
devices whose firmware never answers the status query (Munbyn RW403B —
accepts jobs but never sends a byte back) are skipped, since their
silence would otherwise wrongly mean "printing". The RW403B therefore
has neither a paper pre-check nor roll-end monitoring. How many labels
are still missing cannot be queried — TSPL offers no progress query,
only the binary paper status.

**Form length priming (CUPS fallback only):** the CUPS driver
(`rastertolabel`, EPL2) sends only `N`/`q<width>`/`GW…`/`P1` per label,
but **no form length** — the printer takes it from its stored state.
This setting is volatile on e.g. the Vevor Y486: after switching off it
falls back to a default that rarely matches the inserted roll, and the
printer then feeds several blank labels after each label.
`send_images_to_thermal_printer()` therefore calls
`prime_thermal_printer_geometry()` before every CUPS job, which
transfers the label height plus a 2 mm default gap (EPL2
`Q<height>,<gap>` in dots) directly via `/dev/usb/lp*` — only to devices
that have previously answered the TSPL status query.

**180° rotation:** on request, `render_label_scene()` rotates the
content by half a turn (`rotate_180`, from
`LabelLayoutPreset::rotate_180` or the checkbox "Rotate output by 180°"
in the layout editor) — for printers whose feed direction prints upside
down. The calibration is applied after the rotation, so it stays in
printer coordinates.

**Printing without a dialog:** `QPrinter::setFullPage(true)` and
`QPageSize(mm, Millimeter)` set the exact label size; margins are set
to 0. If the CUPS driver does not accept arbitrary page sizes,
`render_label_scene()` + `lp -o PageSize=Custom.…` can be used as an
alternative.

Interactive test program: `colman_test_label_printer` – select a
printer, enter label dimensions and DPI, enter text and a barcode
string, show a preview and print on the selected printer. It also shows
all `/dev/usb/lp*` devices with their paper status (to verify the
paper-out detection on the real device).

---

@page page_tutorial_screenshots Tutorial screenshots

The tutorial lives per language in `doc/tutorial/<lang>/`:

| File | Contents |
|---|---|
| `colman_tutorial.dox` | Tutorial text of the language |
| `sample_data.json` | Sample data (collection, classes, persons, items, lendings, input of the scenes) |
| `screenshots/*.jpg` | Generated screenshots; the file names are the same in all languages (e.g. `050_lending_01.jpg`) |

The screenshots are generated with
@code
tools/tutorial_screenshots.sh --lang de      # or: --lang en
cmake --build bld --target tutorial_screenshots_de
@endcode
The script sets the locale (`LC_ALL`, `LANG`, `LANGUAGE`), uses the Qt
platform `offscreen` by default (`--display` renders visibly on the
desktop in the native style, `--network` additionally captures images
that depend on the internet) and starts
`colman --tutorial-screenshots=<dir> --tutorial-data=<json>`.

`colman_tutorial_screenshots.cpp` (`run_tutorial_screenshots()`)
creates a new collection with the real start wizard, inserts the sample
data partly through the UI and partly directly via `IQueries`, and runs
through the tutorial chapters one after another.  The following applies:

- QSettings (including Qt's own settings) are stored in a
  `QTemporaryDir`; the user's configuration remains untouched.
  Native dialogs are disabled so that file dialogs can be controlled.
- Widgets are found independently of the language: via the
  `MainWindowUi` and `AppData` members or via `objectName` (convention:
  snake_case with a trailing `_`, e.g. `create_collection_next_`,
  `login_password_`). New scenes may need additional `setObjectName()`
  calls.
- Modal dialogs (`exec()`) block the caller; a watcher timer hands every
  new modal dialog to the handler registered with `expect_dialog()` /
  `click_dialog()`.  Unexpected dialogs are reported, saved as
  `unexpected_dialog.jpg` and closed.
- If a step fails, the program exits with exit code 1, names the step
  and saves `failed_step.jpg`.

New language: create `doc/tutorial/<lang>/` with `sample_data.json`
(and later `colman_tutorial.dox`), and add the language to
`tools/tutorial_screenshots.sh` (locale) and to `CMakeLists.txt`
(`COLMAN_TUTORIAL_LANGS`, Doxygen output language, tip/note texts,
screenshot target).

---

@page page_localization Localization

German translations are in `localization/colman_de.ts` (source) and
`colman_de.qm` (compiled). Qt's own translations are loaded at runtime
from the Qt system directory.

The `.qm` file is generated automatically from the `.ts` source during
the CMake build via `qt6_add_translation()` and embedded as a Qt
resource in the executables -- in the desktop app, the REST client and
also in the REST server (`colman_rest_server`).  Like the desktop app,
the server installs the translations according to the system language
(`QLocale::system()`, i.e. `LANGUAGE`/`LANG`), so that data it creates
-- such as the default signature categories of a new collection -- is
created in that language.  REST responses themselves are not affected.

Update the translation sources (extract new/changed `tr()` calls):
@code
cmake --build bld --target lupdate_colman
@endcode

After the build, a POST_BUILD script (`cmake/check_lupdate.cmake`)
checks via a timestamp comparison whether source files are newer than
the `.ts` file and, if so, prints a hint with the exact command to run.

Since `QT_NO_KEYWORDS` is set, Qt macros must be used in their `Q_`
form: `Q_EMIT`, `Q_SIGNALS`, `Q_SLOTS`.

---

@page page_coding_style Coding style quick reference

Full rules: `colman_coding_style.hpp/.cpp`.
Automatic formatting: `.clang-format` (via `clang-format-17`).

- **Indentation:** 2 spaces, no tabs, 80-character line width
- **Namespace:** everything in `namespace colman`, no `using namespace`
- **East const:** `int const&` instead of `const int&`
- **Classes/types:** CamelCase (`Item`, `Person`)
- **Functions/variables:** snake_case (`get_item()`, `item_id`)
- **Private members:** snake_case + trailing underscore (`name_`)
- **File-local constants:** CamelCase in an anonymous namespace
- **No global/file-local variables:** only constants at file scope;
  mutable state belongs in class members or function parameters
- **Includes:** corresponding header first; guard pattern
  (`#ifndef SYSTEM_QSTRING / #include / #define`)
- **Braces:** Allman for classes/functions; same line for a single-line
  condition (`if(x){`)

*/
